          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Verdict</a>
          <a href="#who-is-quest" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Who Quest Is</a>
          <a href="#blend" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Why Whey Plus Casein</a>
          <a href="#flavors" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Every Flavor, Compared</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Range, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#weight-loss" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Quest for Weight Loss</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cost per 25 g</a>
          <a href="#ingredients" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Sweeteners and Additives</a>
          <a href="#vs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Quest vs Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip It</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Quest Protein Powder is a sensible weight-loss powder with a reasonable price, and Vanilla Milkshake is the flavor to buy. Its whey isolate and micellar casein blend gives about 24 g of protein for about 110 calories in vanilla, with 1 g of sugar, which is as lean as many pure isolates. But protein per scoop ranges from about 20 g to about 26 g across the line, and Quest's own site showed every powder as sold out when we checked, so where you buy matters as much as what you buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict: Lean, Thick and Fairly Priced
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quest Protein Powder does one thing well: it delivers a lot of protein for very few calories without tasting like a compromise, according to the bulk of buyer feedback. Vanilla Milkshake lists 24 g of protein in a 31 g scoop for about 110 calories, with 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and essentially no fat. Salted Caramel goes further on protein at about 26 g per 34 g scoop for about 120 calories. Those are isolate-level numbers from a product that uses casein to make shakes thicker and more filling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The caveats are flavor spread and availability. Cinnamon Crunch drops to about 20 g of protein per scoop, Chocolate Milkshake sits at about 22 g, and Peanut Butter is about 23 g for 120 calories. If you pick by taste alone, you might get 20 percent less protein per scoop than you assumed. And when we checked, Quest's direct store listed every powder as sold out, so most buyers will be shopping at grocery chains, Vitacost, Walmart or Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            On cost, Quest lands in the affordable middle. At Quest's list prices, the 3 lb tub works out to roughly $1.30 per 25 g of protein and the 1.6 lb tub to roughly $1.80, approximately, and grocery prices we saw were a little lower than list for the small tub. That is cheaper than most premium isolates and a little dearer than budget whey blends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our label-based verdict: buy Vanilla Milkshake in the 3 lb size if you want Quest for weight loss, buy Salted Caramel if you want the most protein per scoop, and skip Cinnamon Crunch unless you love the flavor. Tested athletes and sucralose avoiders should look elsewhere. Everything here is built from labels, retailer listings and published research; we did not taste or test the powders ourselves.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy Vanilla Milkshake, in the big tub.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It has the best protein-per-calorie ratio in the range at about 24 g for 110 calories, the lowest cost per 25 g in the 3 lb size, and the most uses beyond shakes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-quest" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Quest Is and What It Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quest Nutrition built its name on protein bars in the early 2010s and expanded into chips, cookies, ready-to-drink shakes and powder. Since 2019 it has been owned by The Simply Good Foods Company, which also owns Atkins, so this is a large packaged-food brand rather than a gym-floor supplement company. That shows in the distribution: Quest powder sits on grocery shelves at chains such as Harris Teeter, Ralphs, Stop and Shop and Giant as well as online.
          </p>

          <p>
            The powder range is one formula family in five current flavors on Quest's own site: Vanilla Milkshake, Chocolate Milkshake, Peanut Butter, Salted Caramel and Cinnamon Crunch. Sizes are 1.6 lb for every flavor and 3 lb for Vanilla Milkshake, Chocolate Milkshake and Peanut Butter, plus multi-tub bundles. Quest has sold other variants over the years, including an unflavored mix, but we only cover what Quest currently lists.
          </p>

          <p>
            When we checked Quest's direct store, every powder and every size was marked sold out. That does not mean the product is discontinued; grocery and online retailers were still listing it. But it does mean you should not count on the brand's subscription discount, and you should compare prices across retailers rather than defaulting to the brand site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quest does not market its powder around sport certifications, and we found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for it. The labels do carry gluten-free and kosher claims. For most dieters that is fine, but if you are drug-tested in competition, the certification gap is a deal-breaker, and our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate rankings</a> include certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Whey Plus Casein Matters for Dieters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most diet-friendly powders are pure whey isolate, which digests quickly and mixes thin. Quest takes a different route: a blend of whey protein isolate and micellar casein, with milk protein isolate added in some flavors such as Chocolate Milkshake and Peanut Butter. Retail listings describe the Vanilla blend as about 60 percent whey isolate and 40 percent micellar casein, with the whey framed as fast-absorbing and the casein as slow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why does that matter? Casein forms a gel in the stomach and digests more slowly than whey, which produces a longer, flatter rise in blood amino acids. Studies comparing the two suggest casein-containing shakes can feel more filling for some people, though the effect on actual calorie intake later in the day is modest and varies between studies. The practical upside is texture: casein makes a thicker, more milkshake-like drink, which many dieters find more satisfying than a watery isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are trade-offs. Casein blends mix thicker and can clump if you use too little liquid, and the slower digestion makes no meaningful difference to muscle growth when total daily protein is adequate. If you specifically want a slow protein before bed, a dedicated micellar casein will deliver more of it; our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein protein powder guide</a> covers those.
          </p>

          <p>
            For baking and protein recipes, the casein content is an advantage. Casein-heavy blends tend to hold moisture better than pure whey, which is why blends like Quest's are popular in protein pancakes, mug cakes and puddings. If you bake with your protein, Vanilla Milkshake is the most flexible flavor to do it with.
          </p>
        </div>

