        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sweeteners, Additives and Allergens
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Quest powder we checked is sweetened with a combination of sucralose and stevia. That is how it keeps sugar at about 1 g per scoop while still tasting sweet. If you avoid sucralose, Quest is not for you; if you are fine with it, the sweetener combination is common across diet-focused powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond sweeteners, the labels list a handful of texture and stability ingredients in amounts under 2 percent: lecithin, cellulose gum, xanthan gum and salt in most flavors, with silicon dioxide and tapioca syrup in Peanut Butter. Vanilla Milkshake also lists carrageenan, a seaweed-derived thickener that some buyers prefer to avoid. Cinnamon Crunch adds erythritol, soluble corn fiber and coconut oil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens are consistent: every flavor contains milk and soy, the soy coming from lecithin, and Peanut Butter adds peanuts. Quest's labels note that the products are made in a facility that also processes eggs, peanuts, sesame, wheat, tree nuts and fish. Anyone with a serious allergy should read the full statement on the tub and, if in doubt, check with their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because Quest uses whey isolate alongside casein and milk protein isolate, it is not as low in lactose as a pure isolate. Many people tolerate it fine, but if dairy reliably upsets you, a pure isolate is a safer test. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate explainer</a> covers why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Quest vs Isopure, Casein and Other Diet Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Isopure Zero Carb</strong> is the leaner alternative: about 25 g of protein for roughly 100 to 110 calories with essentially no carbohydrate, from pure whey isolate. It mixes thinner and contains no casein, so it feels less filling, but it is lower in lactose and slightly more efficient per calorie than any Quest flavor. Our <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure review</a> covers the line.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A dedicated micellar casein</strong> such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein is the alternative if what you like about Quest is the thick texture and slow digestion. It gives you more casein per scoop, which suits an evening shake or a protein pudding. It is usually a little higher in calories per gram of protein than Quest Vanilla.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mainstream whey blends</strong> such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey cost a little less per 25 g and are promoted as Informed Choice certified, but they contain whey concentrate and are not quite as lean. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> compares the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quest's niche is the middle of these three: leaner than a whey blend, thicker than an isolate, cheaper than most premium powders, and available in the grocery aisle. If that is the combination you want, it is hard to beat. If one factor matters much more than the others, one of the alternatives probably fits better.
          </p>
        </div>

